WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:04.055
<v Examiner>The retina: rods, cones, and colour blindness.

2
00:00:04.505 --> 00:00:07.266
<v Examiner>What receptors are impacted by colour blindness?

3
00:00:07.266 --> 00:00:11.119
<v Examiner>And what are the receptors for light, where exactly are they found,

4
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:12.441
<v Examiner>and how do they differ?

5
00:00:12.891 --> 00:00:18.150
Her checklist puts it like this: the physiology of vision includes how the retina works,

6
00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:22.332
and colour blindness is related to the function of the photoreceptors.

7
00:00:22.332 --> 00:00:26.395
Her student-marked quiz asks the colour-blindness part straight out.

8
00:00:26.845 --> 00:00:30.034
The hook: cones for colour and detail, in the centre.

9
00:00:30.034 --> 00:00:32.200
Rods for dim light, round the edges.

10
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:37.473
The receptors for light are photoreceptors: rods and cones, in the retina,

11
00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:39.233
the inner layer of the eye.

12
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:44.447
They contain visual pigments, and they convert light energy into nerve impulses.

13
00:00:44.897 --> 00:00:49.613
Cones sit in the macula lutea, packed most tightly in the fovea centralis,

14
00:00:49.613 --> 00:00:51.971
the very centre of your visual field.

15
00:00:51.971 --> 00:00:55.475
They respond to bright light, which is photopic vision.

16
00:00:55.475 --> 00:00:59.235
They give colour vision, and they form the sharpest images.

17
00:00:59.235 --> 00:01:04.524
Her key: focusing an object on the fovea centralis gives the highest visual acuity.

18
00:01:04.974 --> 00:01:06.461
Rods are more numerous.

19
00:01:06.461 --> 00:01:09.371
They're absent from the fovea and the macula,

20
00:01:09.371 --> 00:01:13.186
and there are more of them towards the edges of the retina.

21
00:01:13.186 --> 00:01:18.876
They respond to dim light, which is scotopic vision, and they see black, white and grey,

22
00:01:18.876 --> 00:01:19.846
with no colour.

23
00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:22.044
Why are rods sensitive but blurry?

24
00:01:22.044 --> 00:01:26.764
Her key: as many as a hundred rods can connect to a single ganglion cell.

25
00:01:26.764 --> 00:01:28.833
Lots of signal, not much detail.

26
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:32.814
A few one-mark details from her quizzes.

27
00:01:32.814 --> 00:01:36.231
Visual pigments are told apart by their opsin protein.

28
00:01:36.231 --> 00:01:39.015
Light makes the photoreceptor hyperpolarise,

29
00:01:39.015 --> 00:01:42.306
and that's what's ultimately signalled to the brain.

30
00:01:42.306 --> 00:01:47.811
The part of the retina with no photoreceptors at all is the optic disc, the blind spot,

31
00:01:47.811 --> 00:01:51.797
not the macula: her quiz offers the macula as the wrong answer.

32
00:01:51.797 --> 00:01:56.985
And an afterimage is when you can still see something after the stimulus has gone,

33
00:01:56.985 --> 00:01:59.327
because the retina's signal persists.

34
00:01:59.777 --> 00:02:03.365
Now her model answer for colour blindness, word for word.

35
00:02:03.365 --> 00:02:08.212
The receptors impacted are photoreceptors, specifically the cones of the eye.

36
00:02:08.212 --> 00:02:11.171
The cones are found mostly in the macula lutea,

37
00:02:11.171 --> 00:02:15.641
the central part of the retina that is the centre of your visual field,

38
00:02:15.641 --> 00:02:19.544
and the fovea centralis, the very centre of your visual field,

39
00:02:19.544 --> 00:02:21.684
where your vision is the clearest.

40
00:02:21.684 --> 00:02:27.035
In red-green colour blindness, it's the L cones, for red, and the M cones, for green.

41
00:02:27.035 --> 00:02:31.316
And it's X-linked recessive, which is why it's more common in males.

42
00:02:31.766 --> 00:02:32.302
<v Examiner>Once more.

43
00:02:32.302 --> 00:02:34.070
<v Examiner>The retina, and colour blindness.

44
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.030
The photoreceptors are rods and cones, in the retina.

45
00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:45.528
Cones sit in the macula lutea, packed in the fovea centralis: bright light, colour,

46
00:02:45.528 --> 00:02:47.117
and the sharpest vision.

47
00:02:47.117 --> 00:02:53.078
Rods are absent from the fovea and increase towards the edges: dim light, black and white,

48
00:02:53.078 --> 00:02:55.595
and many rods share one ganglion cell.

49
00:02:55.595 --> 00:03:00.165
Pigments differ by their opsin, and the optic disc is the blind spot.

50
00:03:00.165 --> 00:03:02.484
Colour blindness affects the cones,

51
00:03:02.484 --> 00:03:07.848
found mostly in the macula lutea and the fovea centralis: in red-green blindness,

52
00:03:07.848 --> 00:03:09.041
the L and M cones.
